# Breeze RMM mobile — build-time public config.
#
# Everything here is compiled INTO the app bundle by Expo (that is what the
# EXPO_PUBLIC_ prefix means). Never put a secret in this file or in any .env
# that feeds it — an API key here ships to every device and is trivially
# recoverable from the IPA.
#
# For a release build these must be present in the environment that runs
# `expo prebuild` / the Xcode Archive, not just in a local shell. See
# STORE_SUBMISSION.md.

# --- Crash + error reporting -------------------------------------------------
# Sentry DSN for the MOBILE project. This is NOT the API project's DSN — mixing
# them makes mobile crashes unfindable among server events.
#
# Sentry self-disables when this is empty (App.tsx gates on
# `!!SENTRY_DSN && !__DEV__`). That is a silent no-op: a TestFlight build
# shipped without it reports NOTHING, and failures that are otherwise
# invisible — approver-device registration is the standing example — become
# undiagnosable. The breeze-mobile Sentry project recorded ZERO events in 90
# days for exactly this reason.
#
# A RELEASE BUILD WITHOUT THIS NOW FAILS. `app.config.js` calls
# `resolveSentryDsn()` (src/config/sentryDsn.js), which throws when it detects a
# release build — Xcode CONFIGURATION=Release, any non-development EAS profile,
# or NODE_ENV=production — and no usable DSN. Expo evaluates app.config.js from
# an expo-constants Xcode build phase on every build, so ⌘B and Product →
# Archive both hit it. Plain local dev, `expo start`, `expo prebuild` and CI are
# untouched.
#
# Note `expo start --no-dev` DOES require a DSN: Expo sets NODE_ENV=production
# for it, and it genuinely produces a __DEV__=false bundle. That is the point of
# the gesture. Use BREEZE_MOBILE_DEV=1 (below) for a throwaway one.
#
# Where the value comes from:
#   • Local Xcode build (the current release path) — put it in this file's real
#     counterpart, `apps/mobile/.env`. It has to be in the FILE: Xcode build
#     phases do not inherit your interactive shell, so exporting it in .zshrc
#     and pressing Archive does not work.
#   • EAS build — `eas.json` maps each profile to the EAS environment of the
#     same name; store the DSN there, not in git. Use the environment matching
#     the profile you build — `preview` needs its own, it is not covered by
#     `production`:
#       eas env:create --environment production \
#         --name EXPO_PUBLIC_SENTRY_DSN --value <dsn> --visibility sensitive
#
# Get it from Sentry → olivetech-ks → breeze-mobile → Settings → Client Keys
# (DSN). Placeholder-looking values (REPLACE_ME, changeme, example.com, TODO, …)
# are rejected by the guard, so a half-configured build fails loudly instead of
# posting events into the void.
#
# --- Escape hatches (leave BOTH unset for anything you intend to ship) -------
# Each takes the literal string `1` and nothing else (matching
# scripts/preflight.mjs), so a near-miss like `=true` fails safe by leaving the
# guard ON. Both print a warning to stderr when they actually suppress
# something, because a safety check that can be switched off silently is not a
# safety check.
#
#   BREEZE_MOBILE_ALLOW_NO_SENTRY=1
#     Build a release deliberately WITHOUT telemetry. The build succeeds and
#     warns. Nothing that build does will ever appear in Sentry.
#
#   BREEZE_MOBILE_DEV=1
#     ⚠️ DISABLES THE RELEASE CHECK ENTIRELY. Same flag scripts/preflight.mjs
#     uses to downgrade its failures to warnings for a local build. It makes
#     this guard treat a genuine Release archive as a dev build, so an IPA can
#     be produced with no crash reporting at all — the precise failure that cost
#     90 days of blind releases. It warns on stderr whenever it suppresses a
#     real release signal, but NOTHING stops that IPA from being uploaded.
#     Never leave it in `apps/mobile/.env`: that file is loaded by the Xcode
#     build phase on every build, including Archive.
EXPO_PUBLIC_SENTRY_DSN=

# The DSN only makes events ARRIVE. Making them READABLE needs source maps and
# dSYMs uploaded at build time, which needs a SENTRY_AUTH_TOKEN — a real secret,
# and therefore NOT here (this file's values are compiled into the bundle).
# It belongs in `.env.sentry-build-plugin`; see that file's .example.

# --- Product analytics -------------------------------------------------------
# Also gated on !__DEV__. Leaving this empty disables PostHog entirely.
# If set, the App Store Connect privacy questionnaire must declare Email
# Address and Product Interaction as linked to identity (see App.tsx).
EXPO_PUBLIC_POSTHOG_KEY=
EXPO_PUBLIC_POSTHOG_HOST=https://us.i.posthog.com

# --- API ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# Fallback API base URL used only until the user picks a server on the
# ServerSelect screen. In a release build this should point at a real region,
# never localhost — a shipped build that falls back to http://localhost:3001
# fails every request with no useful error.
#
# ENFORCED AT BUILD TIME: a release build (Xcode Release / any EAS profile but
# `development` / NODE_ENV=production) FAILS if this is unset, points at
# loopback, points at a private-network address, or uses plaintext http to a
# public host. See src/config/apiUrl.js. Dev builds are untouched.
# Building for a self-hosted server on a LAN? Set
# BREEZE_MOBILE_ALLOW_PRIVATE_API_URL=1. That accepts a private-network host,
# plaintext http included, and warns on every build that it did. It does NOT
# accept loopback, a placeholder, or plaintext to a PUBLIC host. For a
# Release-configuration build you are not shipping at all,
# BREEZE_MOBILE_DEV=1 turns the release guards off (loudly).
EXPO_PUBLIC_API_URL=https://us.2breeze.app
